# Never commit real keys. Set these in Netlify > Site configuration > Environment variables.

# ── Contact form ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Without at least one delivery channel the form reports a real failure instead
# of pretending to send, and the visitor gets a mailto with their message
# already in it. Two channels, either one is enough:
#
#   1. Resend — set RESEND_API_KEY below (resend.com, free tier is 3,000/month),
#      then redeploy. This is the one that actually emails you: a formatted
#      email with the visitor's name, address and message, and Reply answers
#      them (reply_to is the visitor).
#
#   2. Netlify Forms — no key, and confirmed working in production: a POST to
#      /__forms.html comes back with Netlify's "Thank you!" page, so every
#      submission is archived under Forms > contact. It depends on two things
#      that are easy to miss — Site configuration > Forms > form detection must
#      be ON, and a deploy has to run *after* it is switched on so the parser
#      gets a chance to read public/__forms.html. Archiving is not emailing:
#      add a notification under Forms > Notifications to get the message
#      in your inbox.
#
# Receipts: once a message has gone, the visitor gets a short "Thanks for your
# message" from CONTACT_FROM, with your address (CONTACT_TO's first) as
# reply-to. It repeats nothing they typed except their first name, so the form
# can't be used to mail a stranger someone else's words. Receipts are on only
# when RESEND_API_KEY is set AND CONTACT_FROM is on your own domain: Resend's
# shared sender (onboarding@resend.dev) only delivers to the Resend account's
# own address, so it can bring you the message but can't send a receipt.
#
# Setting it all up (the domain uses Netlify DNS):
#   1. resend.com > API Keys > Create API key (sending access is enough).
#   2. resend.com > Domains > Add domain > romankucheryavyy.com. Resend lists
#      the DNS records it needs: an MX and a TXT (SPF) on a "send" subdomain,
#      and a TXT (DKIM) at resend._domainkey. Copy names and values exactly as
#      Resend shows them.
#   3. Netlify > Domains > romankucheryavyy.com > DNS settings > Add new record,
#      once per record (Netlify appends .romankucheryavyy.com to the name
#      itself). They sit on subdomains, so they don't touch any other mail.
#      A DMARC record (TXT at _dmarc, e.g. "v=DMARC1; p=none;") helps the
#      receipts land in inboxes rather than spam.
#   4. Back in Resend, Verify the domain; DNS can take a while to show up.
#   5. Netlify > Site configuration > Environment variables: set
#      RESEND_API_KEY, CONTACT_TO and CONTACT_FROM (an address on the verified
#      domain; it needs no mailbox, replies go to reply-to), then redeploy.
#   6. Netlify's function logs then start with
#      "[env] contact: resend, netlify-forms · receipts: on". Send a message
#      from the site using another address of yours to see both emails.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_your_key_here
CONTACT_TO=kucheryavyyroman@gmail.com
# While it is on resend.dev, mail reaches only the Resend account's own address
# and there are no receipts. After step 4, for example:
#   CONTACT_FROM=Roman Kucheryavyy <hello@romankucheryavyy.com>
CONTACT_FROM=Portfolio <onboarding@resend.dev>

# URL and DEPLOY_PRIME_URL are not set here: Netlify provides them at runtime,
# and the Netlify Forms channel only runs when one of them exists. Leave them
# unset locally, or the contact route will post to that origin.

# ── Resume ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unlocks the /resume builder. Without it every PIN is refused.
RESUME_PIN=your-pin-here

# Claude, for the builder's tailor and cover-letter tools
# (/api/resume/tailor, /api/resume/cover-letter). Without it both answer
# "AI is not configured on the server". Each call bills this account.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-your-key-here
